  "response": "P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(¬D) = 0.998\nP(+|D) = 0.98 (sensitivity)\nP(+|¬D) = 1 − 0.95 = 0.05 (false positive rate)\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem:\n\nP(D|+) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / [P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)]\n= (0.98 × 0.002) / (0.98 × 0.002 + 0.05 × 0.998)\n= 0.00196 / (0.00196 + 0.0499)\n= 0.00196 / 0.05186\n≈ 0.037794 → **3.78%**\n\nSo even with a positive test, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — a classic illustration of how a low base rate can dominate seemingly good test accuracy.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
